        <h2 id="what-does-all-keys-lost-actually-mean" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">What Does "All Keys Lost" Actually Mean?</h2>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-0"><strong class="text-[#1A1F22]">What is an "all keys lost" situation?</strong> An all keys lost job is a key replacement where no working key exists to copy. The locksmith builds a new key from the car's VIN or key code, then programs it to the car's computer.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Locksmiths have a name for it because it's a different animal from copying a spare. Got a spare? A clone takes minutes. Got nothing? The work starts at the car itself.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Sounds scary, but it isn't. Losing your only key happens all the time, and in most cases it gets sorted the same day, as long as someone can reach the car and you can show it's yours.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-5-item-lost-key-kit" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">The 5-Item Lost Key Kit</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Pull these five things together before anyone touches your car. We call it the 5-Item Lost Key Kit, and having it ready is the quickest way to cut the job short.
        </p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Your photo ID.</strong> A driver's license does the trick.</li>
          <li><strong>Proof the car is yours.</strong> The registration card or the title will do. Registration card gone too? The Delaware DMV can issue a duplicate, and its <a href="https://www.dmv.de.gov/VehicleServices/registration/index.shtml?dc=ve_reg_duplicate" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">duplicate registration card page</a> lists proof of insurance and your plate number as what you need to provide.</li>
          <li><strong>The VIN.</strong> Crouch outside the driver's side and peek through the windshield at the dash corner. That 17-character number is it. It's also on your insurance card and registration.</li>
          <li><strong>Make, model and year.</strong> Trim levels can change which key a car takes, so don't skip this one.</li>
          <li><strong>Where the car is.</strong> Garage, lot, curb? The locksmith has to get to it.</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Expect a reputable locksmith to ask for your ID and proof of ownership. That's not red tape. It's what stops a stranger from driving off in your car.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-to-replace-lost-car-keys-step-by-step" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">How to Replace Lost Car Keys, Step by Step</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Most cars from the last 25 years or so follow this routine. Here's how it usually plays out.
        </p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>One last look, then stop.</strong> Think back through your day. Bags, pockets, the friend who rode along. If a spare turns up, use it and ignore the rest of this list.</li>
          <li><strong>Pull together your 5-Item Lost Key Kit.</strong> Ten minutes of digging now saves a second visit.</li>
          <li><strong>Decide who does it.</strong> Mobile locksmith or dealer? The next section sets them side by side.</li>
          <li><strong>Get the key cut.</strong> The blank is cut to fit your door and ignition, using the VIN or by reading the lock itself.</li>
          <li><strong>Program it.</strong> The locksmith plugs a programming tool into the car, usually at the port under the dash, and teaches the car to accept the new key.</li>
          <li><strong>Test it all.</strong> Doors, trunk, engine. If it's a fob, mash every button.</li>
          <li><strong>Erase the lost key.</strong> On many vehicles the old key can be removed from the car's memory. After that, the missing key won't start the car even if a stranger picks it up.</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          People forget step seven all the time. Lost that key at the gym? You want it dead.
        </p>

        <h2 id="locksmith-or-dealer-a-side-by-side-look" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Locksmith or Dealer? A Side-by-Side Look</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Either one can fix this. They just go about it differently.
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border border-gray-200">
            <thead class="bg-[#1A1F22] text-white">
              <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold"></th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Mobile locksmith</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Dealership</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-700">
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Where the work happens</td><td class="px-4 py-3">At your car</td><td class="px-4 py-3">At their service bay</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">If the car won't start</td><td class="px-4 py-3">They come to you</td><td class="px-4 py-3">You may need a tow</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Timing</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Often the same visit</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Depends on parts and the schedule</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Key source</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Cut and programmed on site</td><td class="px-4 py-3">May be ordered by VIN</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Best fit</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Most makes and models, urgent jobs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Some newer or specialty models</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A few newer vehicles use security systems only the manufacturer can unlock. If a locksmith says yours is one of them, take their word for it, because an honest one will tell you straight. For most cars rolling around Kent County, though, a locksmith with the right programming tools can finish the job in your driveway.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Not sure who to trust? Read our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-avoid-locksmith-scams/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">how to avoid locksmith scams in Delaware</a> before you dial. The Federal Trade Commission has long told people to <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/node/43494" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">"do the research before you need one"</a>, and it warns that some locksmiths advertising locally may not be local at all.
        </p>

        <h2 id="why-a-new-key-has-to-be-programmed" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Why a New Key Has to Be Programmed</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Your key does two jobs. The metal blade turns the lock. Inside the plastic head sits a tiny chip, the transponder, and it tells the car this key may start the engine.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If the code is wrong, the engine won't start, no matter how smoothly the blade turns. That's why a key from the hardware store counter can open your door and still refuse to start the car.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          There's a reason the system exists. Engine immobilizers help deter car theft. The feature that protects your car is the same one that makes a new key need programming.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Curious about chip keys versus remote fobs? Our post on the <a href="/blog/transponder-key-vs-key-fob-whats-the-difference/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">difference between a transponder key and a key fob</a> breaks it down.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-to-do-if-the-key-is-lost-not-stolen" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">What to Do If the Key Is Lost, Not Stolen</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Lost and stolen aren't the same problem. Maybe you think someone took it. Maybe your car key and house keys share a ring. Either way, treat it as a security issue.
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Erase the old key</strong> from the car during replacement, as in step seven above.</li>
          <li><strong>Think about your house locks.</strong> If your address is on the registration or sitting in the glove box, whoever finds the ring can find your door. <a href="/services/lock-rekeying/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">Rekeying</a> the house locks is a quick fix.</li>
          <li><strong>Call the police</strong> if you believe the key was stolen.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="who-replaces-lost-car-keys-in-dover-and-kent-county" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Who Replaces Lost Car Keys in Dover and Kent County?</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          1st Capital Locksmith is a locally owned and operated, bonded and insured mobile locksmith. We drive out to you in Dover, Camden, Smyrna, Felton, Harrington, Magnolia, Frederica, Cheswold, Clayton, Milford and Leipsic. Our <a href="/services/car-key-replacement/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">car key replacement service</a> handles lost keys, broken keys and spares, and our <a href="/services/key-fob-programming/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">key fob programming</a> page covers remote and push-start fobs.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          We're open Sunday through Thursday 7AM to 10PM and Friday 7AM to 3:45PM. Saturday we're closed, and emergency service is available by appointment. Dover drivers can start at our <a href="/service-areas/dover-de/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">Dover service page</a>. Locked out of the car too? <a href="/blog/what-to-do-when-locked-out-of-car/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">What to do when you're locked out of your car</a>.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Ready to go? Have the VIN and your ID in hand and call (302) 735-0050, or <a href="/contact/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">request a visit</a>. Tell us the make, model, year and where the car sits. We'll explain the job before we begin.
        </p>

